Q: Improving structure of a Java base conversion program A: <p>Welcome to StackOverflow! Good luck with this, your first question.</p>
<p>I'll start with some comments unrelated to what you asked. Well, at least my understanding of what you asked.</p>
<p>Thank you for mentioning that you know about the built in methods, but you want to do this as a learning exercise, so you didn't use them.</p>
<p>I find the inclusion of the user interface methods distracting. If you want us to critique the conversion methods without regard to the user interface, for StackOverflow,  you might want to replace the user interface methods with a test method:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public static void test () {
    steps (&quot;ABC&quot;,16,10);
    steps (&quot;9AB&quot;,16,8);
    steps (&quot;37&quot;, 7, 2);  // error
    steps (&quot;111000110101&quot;, 2, 10);
}
and so on. 
</code></pre>
<p>On the other hand, you might want us to critique the user interface. I assumed you do not</p>
<p>You might want to read about making a <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-reproducible-example">Minimal Reproducible Example</a> . More recommended reading includes the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/tour">Tour</a> and pages it links to.</p>
<p>Now, on to your conversion code. I don't have an answer for improving your object-oriented design.</p>
<p>But, there are Java basics I would like to point out.</p>
<p>I see, after getting the &quot;source&quot; number and base from the user,  you convert to a common, intermediate base. You then convert from the common, intermediate base to the representation in the &quot;destination&quot; base. That is a good idea.</p>
<p>But, here is the surprise: The intermediate base you used isn't decimal! It's binary. See the tutorial on <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/java/nutsandbolts/datatypes.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java Primitive Types</a> .  The types <code>byte</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>int</code>, and <code>long</code> are each binary numbers representing integers. <code>char</code> is special because the compiler generates code to treat it as a text character.</p>
<p>Consider a pair of lines like these:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>int foo = 123;
System.out.println (foo);
</code></pre>
<p>The compiler converts <code>123</code> to binary: <code>0000000001111011</code> When handling the <code>println</code> method, without being informed otherwise, the system assumes the output is to be in &quot;human readable&quot; form. It converts the value in <code>foo</code> to decimal, and to characters the output device can handle. So, it looks to you as if it is in decimal. Although you intended otherwise, your code did use built-in base conversion.</p>
<p>Your code works because the math you used will work regardless of which base is used as an intermediary.</p>
<p>I have two suggestions:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Don't treat base 10 as special.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Your code in <code>toDec</code> and <code>fromDec</code> is different enough to justify keeping them different methods. Especially considering how small they are, don't worry about duplication in those two methods.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Now, I have a question for you:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Why do  you have methods <code>fromDec</code> and <code>fromDecSteps</code>?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Why do you have methods <code>toDec</code> and <code>toDecSteps</code>?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Except for the inclusion of the calls to <code>System.out.println</code> methods, one of a pair does what the other does.</p>
<p>If I may go off-topic again, I would like to comment on the use of <code>StringBuilder</code> in code like this:</p>
<pre><code>static String fromDec(int n, int b) {
        if (n == 0)
            return &quot;0&quot;;
        StringBuilder r = new StringBuilder();
        while (n &gt; 0) {
            r.insert(0, vc(n % b));
            n /= b;
        }
        return r.toString();
    }
</code></pre>
<p>The line <code>r.insert(0, vc(n % b));</code> would likely cause shifting of the entire current contents prior to insertion, or copying current contents to a new <code>StringBuilder</code> , leaving a space at the beginning.  Consider using the <code>r.append(vc(n % b));</code> instead. Then, use<code>return r.reverse().toString()</code>;</p>
<p>By the way, you can estimate the size of the <code>StringBuilder</code>  you will need before creating it:</p>
<pre><code>double size = (int)( Math.floor ( Math.log((double) n ) / Math.log ((double) b)) ) + 1; 
StringBuilder = new StringBuilder ((int) size); 
</code></pre>
